{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"map-set","title":"Map & Set","group":"Collections & Iteration","order":34,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>Map</code> is a key → value store where keys can be <em>anything</em>; <code>Set</code> is a collection of unique values. Both keep insertion order and have O(1) average lookups.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-map-2\"></i>Map</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const m = new Map();\nconst objKey = { id: 1 };\nm.set(\"name\", \"Asha\").set(42, \"number key\").set(objKey, \"object key\");\nconsole.log(m.get(objKey), m.get(42), m.get(\"missing\"));\nconsole.log(m.has(\"name\"), m.size);\nm.delete(42);\nconsole.log(m);\nfor (const [key, value] of m) console.log(key, \"→\", value);\nconsole.log([...m.keys()], new Map(Object.entries({ a: 1 })));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>object key number key undefined\ntrue 3\nMap(2) { 'name' =&gt; 'Asha', { id: 1 } =&gt; 'object key' }\nname → Asha\n{ id: 1 } → object key\n[ 'name', { id: 1 } ] Map(1) { 'a' =&gt; 1 }</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-scale\"></i>Map vs plain object</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>Object</code></th><th><code>Map</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Key types</td><td>Strings and symbols (others are stringified)</td><td>Any value — objects, functions, <code>NaN</code></td></tr><tr><td>Order</td><td>Integer keys first, then insertion</td><td>Pure insertion order</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td><code>Object.keys(o).length</code></td><td><code>m.size</code></td></tr><tr><td>Iteration</td><td>Via <code>Object.entries</code></td><td>Directly iterable</td></tr><tr><td>Accidental keys</td><td>Inherits <code>toString</code>, <code>constructor</code>, <code>__proto__</code> risks</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Frequent add/delete</td><td>Slower</td><td>Optimised for it</td></tr><tr><td>JSON support</td><td>Native</td><td>Convert: <code>Object.fromEntries(m)</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Use a <code>Map</code> for dynamic dictionaries (caches, counts, lookups by id or by object); use an object for fixed-shape records.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-circles\"></i>Set</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const s = new Set([3, 1, 3, 2, 1]);\ns.add(4).add(1);\nconsole.log(s, s.size, s.has(2));\ns.delete(3);\nconsole.log([...s]);\nconsole.log([...new Set(\"mississippi\")].join(\"\"));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Set(4) { 3, 1, 2, 4 } 4 true\n[ 1, 2, 4 ]\nmisp</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-math-symbols\"></i>Set operations (ES2025, Node 22+)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const a = new Set([1, 2, 3, 4]);\nconst b = new Set([3, 4, 5]);\nconsole.log(a.union(b));\nconsole.log(a.intersection(b));\nconsole.log(a.difference(b), a.symmetricDifference(b));\nconsole.log(new Set([3]).isSubsetOf(a), a.isDisjointFrom(new Set([9])));\n// the classic way, still common in interviews:\nconsole.log([...a].filter((x) =&gt; b.has(x)));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Set(5) { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 }\nSet(2) { 3, 4 }\nSet(2) { 1, 2 } Set(3) { 1, 2, 5 }\ntrue true\n[ 3, 4 ]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-equal\"></i>Equality of keys</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const s = new Set([NaN, NaN, 0, -0, {}, {}]);\nconsole.log(s.size);             // NaN once, 0/-0 once, two distinct objects\nconst m = new Map([[[1], \"a\"]]);\nconsole.log(m.get([1]));         // a different array → different key</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>4\nundefined</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When would you use a Map instead of an object?</summary><div><p>When keys are not strings (objects, numbers you don't want stringified), when keys are added and removed frequently, when you need the size or guaranteed insertion order, or when keys come from user input (no prototype pollution risk).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you remove duplicates from an array?</summary><div><p><code>[...new Set(arr)]</code> — O(n), keeps first-occurrence order. For objects by a field: <code>[...new Map(arr.map((o) =&gt; [o.id, o])).values()]</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}